// Established foundations and Session 01 working directions.
const chapters = [
  {
    "id": "overview",
    "label": "The world",
    "title": "A world divided. An origin shared.",
    "lede": "A Sun Elf battlemage. A Dark Elf Moon Warden. A catastrophe that turns inherited distrust toward war—and an ancient power waiting for both sides to fall.",
    "body": "<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"info\"><span class=\"tag\">THE SUN ALLIANCE</span><h3>Power in the bloodline</h3><p>Sun Elves and humans share a solar faith and generations of arranged marriages. Their ruling families are bound together by inheritance, diplomacy, and magical ancestry.</p></div><div class=\"info lunar\"><span class=\"tag\">THE UNDERGROUND ALLIANCE</span><h3>Trust built beneath the earth</h3><p>Dark Elves and dwarves share a vast, integrated metropolis. Once wary neighbors, they built a civilization where elven magic and dwarven engineering are inseparable.</p></div></div><h3>The fragile present</h3><p>The story begins against a tense peace. In the emerging plot, a magical catastrophe is blamed on the rival faction, driving both alliances toward open war. Neither side yet understands who benefits from the bloodshed.</p><div class=\"note\"><p><strong>The central contradiction</strong><br>Sun Elves consider fated bonds sacred. What happens when that sacred bond chooses someone their society has taught them to distrust?</p></div><h3>What remains of the Fae</h3><p>The first elves descended from humans and Fae. Most modern people believe the Fae vanished or died long ago. Their magic survives in elven blood—and in human lineages with Fae ancestry. The latest working direction places living Fae beyond the mortal world, hidden rather than extinct.</p><p>Established worldbuilding sits alongside the latest working story direction. Proposed plot beats and unsettled rules are labeled throughout.</p>"
  },
  {
    "id": "peoples",
    "label": "Peoples & alliances",
    "title": "Different cultures. The same inheritance.",
    "lede": "Sun and Dark Elves are not opposing forces of good and evil. Their differences grew from competing ideas about how to live with their Fae nature.",
    "body": "<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"info\"><span class=\"tag\">SUN ELVES</span><h3>The discipline of light</h3><p>Beautiful, refined, and deeply invested in duty, reputation, and tradition. Their cities favor pale stone, open skies, gardens, symmetry, and monumental towers. They see themselves as the stable continuation of ancient elven civilization.</p></div><div class=\"info lunar\"><span class=\"tag\">DARK ELVES</span><h3>The freedom to change</h3><p>Equally beautiful, they value intuition, emotional honesty, adaptability, and individuality. They embrace the uncertain parts of their inheritance and remember their ancestors as survivors of persecution. The latest direction adds a militant, socially egalitarian character to this communal culture; universal military service remains a possibility.</p></div><div class=\"info\"><span class=\"tag\">HUMANS</span><h3>Dynasties of borrowed magic</h3><p>Allied with Sun Elves through faith and interwoven family trees. Humans have no natural magic without Fae ancestry, making elven marriages extraordinarily valuable to noble houses. Admiration can coexist with resentment of elven influence.</p></div><div class=\"info lunar\"><span class=\"tag\">DWARVES</span><h3>The living stone</h3><p>An ancient subterranean people devoted to Gaia. Stone, metal, roots, fungi, and underground rivers all belong to the living world. Craftsmanship and mining carry spiritual weight. Their friendship with Dark Elves is matched by a long memory of Sun Elf invasions.</p></div></div><h3>The absent ancestors</h3><p>The Fae once lived close enough to humanity to have children together. Those descendants became elves. Most mortal societies do not know why the Fae disappeared. The emerging story will challenge their assumption that disappearance meant extinction.</p>"
  },
  {
    "id": "protagonists",
    "label": "The battlemage & Warden",
    "title": "Two lives. Neither easily left.",
    "lede": "She has fought to remain in the field. He has built his entire identity around service. Choosing one another must cost them both.",
    "body": "<span class=\"status\">CURRENT CHARACTER DIRECTION</span><div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"info\"><span class=\"tag\">THE SUN ELF HEROINE</span><h3>The battlemage</h3><p>A powerful field mage serving alongside human soldiers. Her unit protects her while she unleashes major magic; competence, trust, and care for her team define her life in the field.</p></div><div class=\"info lunar\"><span class=\"tag\">THE DARK ELF HERO</span><h3>The Moon Warden</h3><p>An elite operative serving his people directly: part marshal, investigator, diplomat, and spymaster. Moon Warden is the favored role, with its formal rules still being developed.</p></div></div><h3>Her freedom has a deadline</h3><p>She has extended her assignments to delay returning home. Her political marriage contract is finally coming due, and marriage would end her field career. Her father is a decent man who has delayed as long as he reasonably can. Her intended husband is an excellent political match, not an abusive villain.</p><p>To choose a Dark Elf means breaking an alliance, disappointing a loving father, and risking her standing among her own people. The life she refuses is reasonable on paper—and wrong for her.</p><h3>His authority is his identity</h3><p>A Moon Warden undertakes missions rather than simply commanding agents. In the working concept, Wardens may override ordinary law, requisition resources, negotiate treaties, order executions, and potentially declare war in defense of the realm. They hold no territorial courts.</p><p>Four or five Wardens are under consideration; five could allow a deciding vote. Their selection process, checks on their authority, and exact limits remain open.</p><h3>Faith without romanticism</h3><p>The hero accepts that the Moon goddess exists and may protect her people. He regards their reciprocal relationship pragmatically. Other Wardens may be much more devout, creating tension within the order.</p><div class=\"note\"><p><strong>Who is he without the office?</strong><br>Proposed Warden vows exclude spouses and family to prevent divided loyalties. If those vows are adopted, choosing her means stepping down—not merely changing jobs, but surrendering the purpose he believes he was made for.</p></div><h3>An opening in the aftermath</h3><span class=\"status\">PROPOSED OPENING</span><p>She wins a battle with her human unit. Royal emissaries arrive immediately afterward: her father has ordered her home, and the marriage can no longer be postponed. The confrontation reveals how long she has used field assignments to defer this moment.</p><h3>The emerging story spine</h3><p>A dual point of view is favored, keeping the investigation and emotional center with the romantic leads.</p><div class=\"timeline\"><section><h3>Duty calls them back</h3><p>Introduce her through battle and recall; introduce him through Warden service.</p></section><section><h3>A catastrophe becomes an accusation</h3><p>Both factions blame the other. The leads meet as enemies as their societies move toward war.</p></section><section><h3>Attraction precedes recognition</h3><p>Forced proximity or cooperation brings an unwelcome pull. A dangerous encounter leads to touch, recognition, and possibly an eclipse.</p></section><section><h3>The evidence points elsewhere</h3><p>Neither elven faction caused the crisis. Searching for a way to stop it leads toward lost Fae knowledge and a hidden realm.</p></section><section><h3>The answer becomes a choice</h3><p>Fae guidance is confusing rather than a simple solution. The pair must confront what a shared future asks of both their lives.</p></section></div>"
  },
  {
    "id": "history",
    "label": "The ancient schism",
    "title": "History has more than one witness.",
    "lede": "The first elves belonged to one civilization. A disagreement about their future became a rupture that shaped the world.",
    "body": "<div class=\"timeline\"><section><h3>A shared beginning</h3><p>Human and Fae ancestry produced the first elves, who inherited magic through their blood.</p></section><section><h3>A philosophical divide</h3><p>One tradition sought to discipline Fae nature through order and restraint. Another believed suppressing that nature was dangerous, embracing instinct, dreams, and change.</p></section><section><h3>Belief becomes violence</h3><p>Religious and political identities hardened. An earlier crusade drove the ancestors of the Dark Elves from their lands. Their underground existence began as exile, not a freely chosen withdrawal.</p></section><section><h3>Strangers in dwarven lands</h3><p>Dwarves initially saw the refugees as trespassers. Understanding their flight led to practical cooperation, then trust, then an enduring alliance.</p></section><section><h3>Invasions remembered</h3><p>Sun Elf forces pursued fleeing populations toward dwarven territory. Later rulers claimed underground resources by divine or ancestral right. Dwarves remember invasion; Sun Elf accounts often minimize or justify it.</p></section><section><h3>An uneasy peace</h3><p>Trade and diplomacy survive alongside historical grievances. Neither modern elven civilization possesses the complete truth of the schism.</p></section></div><div class=\"note\"><p><strong>An unresolved chronology</strong><br>The Fae vanished long ago. The exact timing and its relationship to the schism have not been established.</p></div>"
  },
  {
    "id": "divine",
    "label": "Gods & inherited magic",
    "title": "The gods are real. Magic is older.",
    "lede": "The Sun and Moon are complementary forces. Centuries of mortal belief have made their differences seem irreconcilable.",
    "body": "<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"info\"><span class=\"tag\">THE SUN GOD · NAME UNDECIDED</span><h3>Light & order</h3><p>Worshipped by Sun Elves and humans. Associated with light, purity, law, justice, and order. A proposed flaw is excessive rigidity: the impulse to treat even minor transgressions as unforgivable.</p></div><div class=\"info lunar\"><span class=\"tag\">THE MOON GODDESS · NAME UNDECIDED</span><h3>Cycles & possibility</h3><p>Worshipped by Dark Elves. Associated with night, dreams, destiny, change, emotion, magic, and fertility. Her connection to the Moon Wardens is part of the developing story.</p></div></div><h3>Gaia, the living world</h3><p>For dwarves, Gaia is the consciousness of the planet: nature, life, and stone. Gaia is likely older than the solar and lunar traditions, and regards both forces as necessary to the natural world. The exact divine relationships and degree of intervention remain open.</p><h3>One source, two traditions</h3><p>Elven magic comes from inherited Fae blood, not an original gift from the gods. Training and culture shape how that power is expressed.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tradition</th><th>Approach</th><th>Expressions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sun Elf</td><td>Precision, structure, control</td><td>Light, wards, formal spellcraft</td></tr><tr><td>Dark Elf</td><td>Intuition, emotion, adaptation</td><td>Illusion, dreams, transformation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"note\"><p><strong>The divide is cultural, not biological.</strong><br>A Sun Elf can learn Dark Elf magic, and vice versa—a fact that challenges both peoples’ claims to fundamental difference.</p></div><h3>Worship is reciprocal</h3><span class=\"status\">WORKING DIVINE RULES</span><p>Worship strengthens the gods. In return, they offer protection, order, magical gifts, or other benefits. They are demonstrably real and sometimes intervene, but they have biases and flaws. Protecting their people also protects their own strength.</p><div class=\"note\"><p><strong>Inheritance and divine gifts are different questions.</strong><br>Elven magical capacity still comes from Fae ancestry. The new notes also allow divine benefits; exactly how those gifts interact with inherited magic remains to be defined.</p></div><h3>What lies outside the bargain</h3><p>One promising distinction separates newer gods sustained by reciprocal worship from primordial powers sustained by consumption. This may explain the returning enemy’s different rules, but what it consumes—and why the gods cannot simply stop it—remains unresolved.</p>"
  },
  {
    "id": "bloodlines",
    "label": "Bloodlines & belonging",
    "title": "The same struggle. Two answers.",
    "lede": "Full-blooded elves have great difficulty conceiving with one another. This shared reality has shaped marriage, family, and power in very different ways.",
    "body": "<h3>The Sun Elf answer: dynastic planning</h3><p>Elven–human fertility is much closer to the human baseline. Sun Elves frequently enter arranged marriages with human nobility, creating heirs with magical potential and binding ruling families across generations.</p><p>For human houses, Fae ancestry is political capital. Carefully tracked genealogies and selective marriages consolidate magic, prestige, and access to elven leadership. Love may develop, but it is not always the deciding consideration.</p><h3>The Dark Elf answer: shared continuity</h3><p>Dark Elves developed communal fertility traditions rather than relying on widespread intermarriage. The monthly Lunar Masquerade loosens rank and courtship conventions, while children are accepted regardless of whether both biological parents are known.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Sun Elf culture</th><th>Dark Elf culture</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reproduction</td><td>Human intermarriage and dynastic planning</td><td>Communal traditions and lunar celebrations</td></tr><tr><td>Belonging</td><td>Genealogy, inheritance, family ties</td><td>Households, extended kin, community</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty</td><td>Something to organize and control</td><td>Something to accommodate and accept</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"note\"><p><strong>Neither system is inherently superior.</strong><br>Both grew from the same difficulty. The romance asks which inherited values each protagonist will choose to keep.</p></div><span class=\"status\">UNRESOLVED</span><p>The cause of low elven fertility, half-elven fertility, and the way magical inheritance changes across generations are still unknown. Greater fertility between fated mates remains a possible direction, not an established rule.</p><h3>A contract coming due</h3><p>The heroine’s value as a field battlemage has allowed her to postpone an arranged marriage. In the working character setup, her culture favors early marriage to maximize childbearing years; her exceptional usefulness has won a delay until roughly twenty-five. Marriage would mean leaving active fieldwork for a domestic, reproductive role.</p><p>Her father has tried to give her time and find a suitable match. Her intended husband is respectable. She is not escaping a conveniently cruel man: she is resisting a life that serves her family and alliance but does not fit her.</p>"
  },
  {
    "id": "underworld",
    "label": "Life beneath the earth",
    "title": "Beneath the surface, a living world.",
    "lede": "The great underground city belongs to Dark Elves and dwarves together. Its name is still undecided. Its character is unmistakably shared.",
    "body": "<h3>A city built in every direction</h3><p>Elven towers descend from cavern ceilings. Dwarven halls cut into immense stone walls. Suspended bridges cross underground rivers, while geothermal lakes, luminous fungal forests, roots, crystals, and foundries fill the depths.</p><p>Magical transportation and dwarven engineering connect neighborhoods whose traditions have blended for centuries. The city is not a pair of segregated districts. It is one interwoven civilization: intimidating from afar, warm and communal within.</p><div class=\"note\"><p><strong>A nature faith beneath the earth</strong><br>For dwarves, a cavern is as much a part of nature as a forest. The city exists within Gaia’s living world.</p></div><h3>The Lunar Masquerade</h3><p>Once each lunar cycle, Dark Elves hold a masked celebration of fertility, renewal, and the continuation of their people. Masks obscure social rank; for one night, wealth, profession, and lineage are meant to matter less.</p><p>Participation is voluntary and adult. Anonymous or semi-anonymous intimacy and conception are accepted parts of the tradition. Within Dark Elf culture, the Masquerade is a respected institution, not a scandal.</p><h3>Children of the community</h3><p>Children conceived during the Masquerade are fully accepted, even when parentage is uncertain. Extended households and community support can take precedence over strict biological lineage. Specific inheritance rules remain to be developed.</p><p>Dwarven neighbors, mentors, teachers, and honorary relatives form part of Dark Elf children’s everyday lives. Dwarves may join the festivities socially, while the fertility traditions primarily concern Dark Elves.</p>"
  },
  {
    "id": "bond",
    "label": "Fated mates",
    "title": "Recognition is not surrender.",
    "lede": "A rare remnant of Fae magic connects two people across a divide their societies insist should remain intact.",
    "body": "<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"info\"><span class=\"tag\">WHAT THE BOND DOES</span><h3>A profound recognition</h3><p>Emotional resonance. Heightened awareness of one another. Unusual magical compatibility. A powerful sense that the other person matters.</p></div><div class=\"info lunar\"><span class=\"tag\">WHAT THE BOND DOES NOT DO</span><h3>Erase the choice</h3><p>The bond predates modern religion and does not originate with either the Sun or Moon goddess. People can resist it, even if they cannot easily ignore it.</p></div></div><h3>One bond, four interpretations</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><tbody><tr><td>Sun Elves</td><td>A sacred connection that can contradict dynastic plans and religious prejudice.</td></tr><tr><td>Dark Elves</td><td>An ancient Fae inheritance to respect, rather than an unquestionable command.</td></tr><tr><td>Dwarves</td><td>A practical challenge to the idea that politics can overrule an ancient connection.</td></tr><tr><td>Humans</td><td>Views differ; noble houses may resist bonds that disrupt magical marriages.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>The romance at the center</h3><p>One Sun Elf. One Dark Elf. Their connection puts love, ancestry, faith, and obligation into conflict. They bring different assumptions about intimacy, marriage, family, and permanence.</p><span class=\"status\">THE CURRENT CHARACTER DIRECTION</span><p>The heroine’s political marriage is coming due. The hero’s favored role is Moon Warden, and proposed vows would require him to surrender that office to choose a partner. Their names and exact first encounter remain open; their emotional stakes are now sharply defined.</p><div class=\"note\"><p><strong>The question is bigger than whether they belong together.</strong><br>What do they owe the lives and communities they already belong to?</p></div><h3>The touch that reveals it</h3><p>Before recognition, the leads feel an unexplained pull toward an enemy. Attraction, suspicion, and curiosity have time to grow. They cannot identify one another as mates until they physically touch. A rescue during a fight is one possible first contact.</p><span class=\"status\">PROPOSED STORY BEAT</span><h3>The world’s first eclipse</h3><p>That touch may activate the bond and coincide with the world’s first eclipse. A union of the Sun and Moon would carry enormous religious and political meaning. Whether the eclipse is a divine declaration, precisely when it happens, and who understands its cause are still undecided.</p><p>The possibility of a public celestial sign sits alongside a later scene in which the bond remains privately known. Who can connect the eclipse to the pair needs to be resolved.</p><h3>A Warden at the gates</h3><span class=\"status\">POSSIBLE LATER SEQUENCE</span><p>After recognizing the bond, they separate without telling their societies. He later demands entry at the Sun Elf gates as a Moon Warden. His authority has no legal force there, but refusing him could provoke the Dark Elves and their dwarven allies. Admission under guard becomes the safer political choice; the Sun Elves do not know he has come for his mate.</p>"
  },
  {
    "id": "primordial",
    "label": "The primordial threat",
    "title": "Let them destroy one another.",
    "lede": "An ancient power is returning. Before it can fully enter the world, it needs the world to weaken itself.",
    "body": "<span class=\"status\">STRONGEST WORKING VERSION</span><h3>A war with a hidden beneficiary</h3><p>The antagonist is a primordial god or an entity of comparable power. It cannot yet manifest fully. A catastrophe—its precise form still undecided—makes both elven factions believe the other has attacked them.</p><div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"info\"><span class=\"tag\">MORTAL COST</span><h3>Fewer defenders</h3><p>War kills powerful mages, soldiers, and ordinary people, leaving a fractured world less able to resist the entity’s return.</p></div><div class=\"info lunar\"><span class=\"tag\">DIVINE COST</span><h3>Fewer worshippers</h3><p>In the developing worship rules, the death of followers weakens the Sun and Moon gods. Their feud would serve a power outside their reciprocal bargain.</p></div></div><h3>The door opened from within</h3><p>A practitioner seeking greater power may use an ancient spell or ritual and accidentally admit the entity’s essence. The promised power is real; so is the price they do not understand. Whether this person becomes a willing villain, an exploited vessel, or both remains open.</p><h3>Agency under temptation</h3><p>The favored refinement is recruitment or empowerment of people already drawn to violence, ambition, or forbidden magic. Easy, indiscriminate possession and zombie-like corruption received pushback because they erase too much personal agency.</p><h3>An old defeat, not a final death</h3><p>In the leading ancient-history concept, the Fae destroyed the entity’s physical form with an anti-magic weapon—perhaps a special alloy or sword. Centuries or millennia of reconstitution have brought it back toward the world.</p><div class=\"note\"><p><strong>The rules are not yet settled.</strong><br>Its exact nature, appetite, origin, returning ritual, and final means of defeat remain open. So does the reason the established gods cannot defeat it directly.</p></div><details><summary>What still needs an answer?</summary><p>Does it consume souls, life, magic, divine energy, or a combination? Was it dormant here or arriving from another consumed world? What can kill or contain it without making every magical attack feed it? These are development questions, not established abilities.</p></details>"
  },
  {
    "id": "fae",
    "label": "Beyond the vanished Fae",
    "title": "Vanished is not the same as gone.",
    "lede": "The mortal world remembers an absence. The emerging story suggests that, beyond a hidden threshold, the ancestors survived.",
    "body": "<span class=\"status\">STRONGEST WORKING VERSION</span><h3>The people beyond the veil</h3><p>The Fae once defeated the primordial entity, breaking its physical form with an anti-magic weapon. Afterward they disappeared into a separate realm or pocket dimension. Most modern people know they existed, but assume they vanished or died; discovering that they are still alive changes everything.</p><h3>Why remain apart?</h3><p>Possible motives include souls or magic bright enough to attract primordial predators, a hidden realm where those predators cannot find them, or a magical world they prefer to mortal life. The discussion favored a deliberate withdrawal over imprisonment, but the reason is not final.</p><h3>A threshold that recognizes ancestry</h3><span class=\"status\">PROPOSED PORTAL RULE</span><p>The leads may discover the realm while pursuing ancient knowledge about the threat. Their combined magical strength, heritage, and activated mate bond could make them register as sufficiently Fae to enter. Others might locate the threshold without being able to cross it.</p><p>One first-contact possibility has the Fae mistake them for unusual-looking members of their own people who visited the mortal world without permission.</p><h3>Guidance without an easy answer</h3><p>The Fae should not simply hand over a solution. Their guidance may appear misleading, incomplete, or useless while giving the protagonists the information they need to reach the answer themselves. The pair might leave more confused than when they arrived.</p><div class=\"note\"><p><strong>The investigation belongs to the couple.</strong><br>The favored direction keeps the emotional and investigative focus on the two leads. A representative party of every major people, and an accidental discovery while simply running away together, were discussed but not adopted.</p></div>"
  },
  {
    "id": "mysteries",
    "label": "The unanswered",
    "title": "What the world has forgotten.",
    "lede": "Some truths are disputed. Others have not yet been written. These questions remain open in the story bible.",
    "body": "<details open><summary>What is the primordial power?</summary><p>Its exact nature, what it consumes, where it has been, how the ritual admits it, and how it can finally be defeated remain undecided. The gods’ inability to resolve the threat directly also needs a coherent explanation.</p></details><details><summary>Why did the Fae withdraw?</summary><p>The strongest working version places living Fae in another realm after an ancient victory. Deliberate separation is favored, but their reason for withdrawing—and refusing to return—is not final.</p></details><details><summary>Does the first touch cause an eclipse?</summary><p>Touch reveals the mate bond after an earlier unexplained attraction. The first eclipse is a proposed consequence. Its timing, divine meaning, and whether observers recognize the pair as its cause remain open.</p></details><details><summary>What are the limits of a Moon Warden?</summary><p>The number of Wardens, their selection, accountability, authority, and vows need formal definition. Renouncing spouse and family is a proposed obligation that would force the hero to resign to choose his mate.</p></details><details><summary>What catastrophe sets the war in motion?</summary><p>An attack, outbreak, or magical disaster could be mistaken for enemy action. Its exact form and the evidence that exposes the primordial power’s involvement are not yet chosen.</p></details><details><summary>What truly happened during the schism?</summary><p>The Dark Elves were forced underground by a crusade. Modern histories are biased, and the full chronology—especially in relation to the Fae withdrawal—remains incomplete.</p></details><details><summary>What remains unresolved about magic and fertility?</summary><p>The cause of low elven fertility, half-elven fertility, the inheritance of magical strength over generations, and greater fertility between fated mates remain open. So does the precise relationship between inherited magical capacity and divine gifts.</p></details><details><summary>Which names and story choices are still open?</summary><p>Sun Elves remains this compendium’s term while Sun, Light, or High Elf terminology is being considered. Protagonist names, divine names, nation names, and the underground city’s name are not fixed. The Fae investigation, final resolution, and exact first meeting still need to be mapped.</p></details><details><summary>Which directions were set aside?</summary><p>A representative fellowship, an accidental Fae discovery while fleeing together, effortless corruption of anyone, and a cruel father or intended husband used to simplify the heroine’s choice were discussed but not adopted.</p></details>"
  }
];
